# Every layer that denies having a remainder, beside whether its own two numbers imply one. One
# row per denial, `violates` where the layer states both a demand and a nameplate, so the
# remainder it says does not exist is derivable from the filing itself, even where the two
# coincide.
#
# A denial its own figures refute
#   The rule: a layer that denies having a remainder is not contradicted by its own figures. If a
#   layer files a demand and a nameplate, then `r = n - d` is a quantity the document itself
#   supplies and the denial is refuted by the page it is written on. This spans three elements
#   of one layer, which is why no grammar can state it.
#
# No escape for an exact coincidence
#   "The two coincide exactly" does not belong in the predicate. A point nameplate equal to a
#   point demand is `r = 0`, and `Fit` settles that case in prose: "IT IS A CLEARANCE FIT",
#   extending ISO 286's line-to-line answer to the one pair its clauses do not separate. A
#   clearance is a remainder, filed with a sign and a quantity of [0, 0, 0]. Exempting it lets
#   the wrapper's absence stand in for a filing the schema has already decided, which is the last
#   place `none` on a remainder can hide.
#
# A computed remainder is filed, not denied
#   A layer whose remainder the receiver computes files the remainder, and names `magnitude` as
#   what computes its quantity. The denial takes a `pm:ClaimAbsence`, `unmeasured` or
#   `notApplicable`, and a stated pair contradicts either.
#
# Why the rule is not about holders
#   "A denied remainder names no holders and no absorber" would be vacuous by construction:
#   `Holder` and `absorber` live inside the `Remainder` element, so a layer that declined the
#   element cannot carry either. XSD already enforces it structurally and the absorber half is a
#   CHECK in the DDL. A rule that cannot fail examines nothing however many rows it counts, which
#   is the trap this whole file is written to name.
#
# Thin, not vacuous
#   On this corpus both denials file their nameplate as `notApplicable` (a ratio and an object
#   store, neither of which states a nameplate for a demand to be subtracted from), so both pass,
#   and the rule is thin rather than vacuous: it examines real rows and could fail on the next
#   filing.
-- pm:StatedRemainder's absent branch against the layer's own pm:Demand and pm:Nameplate, from layers/figures.sqlc.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT d.filing, d.layer,
           (f.d_low IS NOT NULL AND f.n_low IS NOT NULL)     AS violates,
           CASE WHEN f.d_low IS NULL OR f.n_low IS NULL
                THEN format('`%s`, and no remainder is derivable: %s',
                            d.reason,
                            CASE WHEN f.d_low IS NULL THEN 'no demand is stated'
                                 ELSE 'no nameplate is stated' END)
                ELSE format('`%s`, yet demand [%s, %s] against a nameplate of [%s, %s] '
                            'gives a remainder the filing supplies itself',
                            d.reason, f.d_low, f.d_high, f.n_low, f.n_high)
           END AS detail
    FROM      (
        :compose(layers/denied_remainders.sqlc)
    ) d
    LEFT JOIN (
        :compose(layers/figures.sqlc)
    ) f USING (filing, layer)
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'denied_remainder_is_not_contradicted'
